{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2023",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": ["node"],
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "composite": true,
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.node.tsbuildinfo",
    // The app resolves `@/` and this project did not, so a build-time check
    // could not import the app module implementing the rule it was checking.
    // Rows C1 and W7 both hit it; see the `include` notes.
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": { "@/*": ["./src/*"] }
  },
  "include": [
    "vite.config.ts",
    "vitest.config.ts",
    "eslint.config.js",
    "pipeline",
    "scripts",
    "tools",
    // The build-time pipeline validates its output against the catalog contract,
    // so it imports it. A composite project rejects an import of a file outside
    // its own file list (TS6307), and the app project cannot be referenced
    // because it is noEmit and so publishes no declarations.
    "src/catalog",
    // Row X4 took the two boundary requests rows C1 and W7 each filed against
    // this file, and added one of its own. All three are the same shape: a
    // build-time check that can only be written where the app already
    // implements the rule, and whose alternative is a second copy of it.
    //
    //   - `src/composition` — C1's request. `pipeline/build.ts` can now run
    //     `measureComposition`/`assertComposition` beside A1's aggregation
    //     check. Its only cross-directory import is `@/catalog`, already here.
    //   - `src/assembly/footprint.ts` and `src/assembly/assemblyIndex.ts` —
    //     W7's request, so `pipeline/catalog.test.ts` can compare against
    //     `footprintKey` and `printOption` directly rather than at the coarser
    //     granularity both sides could spell. Named as files rather than as the
    //     directory, exactly as `src/three/stl/parse.ts` is: `resolve.ts` and
    //     `bill.ts` import `@/store`, which pulls zustand and DOM-typed state
    //     into a project that has neither.
    //   - `src/share/manifest.ts` — X4's own. The share manifest is one of the
    //     five artefacts the stamp covers and the only one with no file, so the
    //     stamp step regenerates it from the index and round-trips all 8,702
    //     ordinals. `link.ts` is deliberately not here; it needs
    //     `CompressionStream`.
    //
    // `tools/hygiene/project.test.ts` imports across each of these boundaries
    // and calls what it imports, so removing an entry breaks the typecheck
    // rather than quietly reverting a capability.
    "src/composition",
    "src/assembly/footprint.ts",
    "src/assembly/assemblyIndex.ts",
    "src/share/manifest.ts",
    // Row B2's, and the same shape as the three above: `pipeline/templates.ts`
    // gates every fixture's part-name set against the three slot conventions, so
    // a twenty-first fixture file fails `npm run import:catalog` naming the
    // template rather than reaching the browser with no slot geometry. The
    // alternative is a second copy of the three part-name sets in `pipeline/`.
    //
    // Named as a single file for the reason `src/three/stl/parse.ts` is:
    // `src/template/offsets.ts` needs `@/builder/canvas`, which needs DOM lib.
    // `rules.ts` is deliberately import-free, so this entry pulls in nothing.
    "src/template/rules.ts",
    // Named as a single file, not as the directory: `src/three/stl/` also holds
    // the Worker client, which needs DOM lib this project does not have. The
    // measurement and LOD tools both parse STL, and a second copy of the parser
    // is the only alternative.
    "src/three/stl/parse.ts"
  ]
}
